@flexiarg institutions/user-boundaries
@title User Boundaries
@keywords boundaries, membership, exclusion, legitimate users, Ostrom 1A, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 1A, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/contributor-boundaries]

! conclusion:
  "Clear boundaries between legitimate users and nonusers must be clearly defined."

  + context:
    Common-pool resources (fisheries, forests, irrigation systems) are rivalrous, since one party's use precludes another's, and nonexcludable, since blocking access is hard. The repetition in the wording is deliberate: the boundaries must be both distinct and explicitly defined.

  + IF:
    A group of users shares a resource and some of them invest in maintaining it.

  + HOWEVER:
    Boundaries exclude, and the excluded may have claims of their own; mobile or seasonal users make membership hard to fix.

  + THEN:
    Define who holds rights to withdraw from the resource, make the definition explicit and known to users and nonusers, and keep it current.

  + BECAUSE:
    Without known boundaries, those who restrain their use or invest in the resource cannot be sure that others will not take the benefits.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/contributor-boundaries.]
